Hi:

Your problem has nothing to do with melt() per se; rather, it has to
do with your choice of x scale within each facet. Here's a simple toy
example that uses ggplot() rather than qplot():

library(ggplot2)
DF <- data.frame(country = rep(LETTERS[1:4], each = 50),
                  region = rep(1:2, each = 100),
                  y = rnorm(200))
# ...what you apparently want
ggplot(DF, aes(x = country, y = y)) + geom_boxplot() +
    facet_wrap(~ region, scales = "free_x")
# ...as opposed to what you got
ggplot(DF, aes(x = country, y = y)) + geom_boxplot() +
    facet_wrap(~ region)

I don't use qplot() very often, but I imagine you'd have to declare it
as a separate layer rather than within the qplot() call in order to
specify the scales = argument.

Dennis

